      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Quayholt Insurance, a fictional company. Five web pages are open, each one reached from a link in a Quayholt inbox. Some are real and some were built to collect your password. For each one, decide whether you'd sign in, and report anything that doesn't sit right. Reporting when you're unsure is always fine and never marked down, though a real one does take a colleague's time to check.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "The page passed without a slip this time, nothing in it to pull apart. Next, the same kind of page turns up behind a link in a Quayholt inbox, and you're the one deciding whether to sign in.",
        "noBlameLine": "A miss here is no reflection on you. Every one of these pages was built to pass for the real thing, and signing in to one says nothing about how careful a person is.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: before you type a password, read the address bar. Your organisation's real sign-in page sits on its own domain, and your password manager knows it. If a page asks you to sign in and the address is new to you, close it, go to the site the way you normally would, and report the link using the route in the box below.",
        "defendOnly": "These pages were built to pass for the real sign-in, and anyone can be caught by one. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "defendIntro": "Five pages are open, each from a link. For each one, decide whether you'd sign in, then tick what you noticed.",
        "conceptPanel": {
          "heading": "If a password goes in",
          "text": "When a password is typed into a lookalike page, the real account is taken, and IT spend the day on a forced reset, a mailbox-rule review and a check of which claimant records were reached. A claims account holds health and other special-category data, so under UK GDPR the organisation assesses the breach without undue delay and, where it has to report, tells the ICO within 72 hours; claimants are told where the risk to them is high."
        }
      },
      "debrief": {
        "mondayCard": "slot:customer_report_route",
        "noBlameLine": true
      }
    },
    "awareness": {
      "ruleOfThumb": {
        "heading": "The habit to keep",
        "text": "When a message pressures you to move money, grant access, sign in or read back a code, stop and check it yourself through a route you already trust, not the link, number or page the message gives you. Taking a minute to confirm is always a fair thing to do."
      }
    }
